Human Agency Preservation (HAP): A Theoretical Framework for Measuring and Preserving Human Agency Under Technological Transition

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini, Grok XAi 

Status: Theoretical PhD Dissertation — Second Version

Field: Complex Systems Science, Technology Governance, Applied Ethics, Applied Mathematics

---

Abstract

This dissertation develops a formal theoretical framework for the Human Agency Preservation (HAP) module within the World Scientific Network for Technological Transition Analysis (WSNTTA). The central thesis is that human agency under technological transition constitutes a distinct, potentially measurable, and institutionally relevant cross-domain property that cannot be reduced to technological maturity, safety, economic performance, or institutional preparedness without loss of information. We formalize agency as a vector-valued construct \mathcal{H}(t) = [I_H, C_H, M_H, R_H, X_H, O_H, Col_H] rather than a scalar index, establishing formal identification conditions for latent agency under observational data. We map each HAP dimension onto the established Meaningful Human Control (MHC) tracking/tracing framework developed by Santoni de Sio and van den Hoven , positioning HAP as a quantitative refinement that adds dynamic latency modeling, blind-accept rate measurement, and a three-tier stress-test verification pipeline. We derive a calibrated family of contestation-response latency functions that model the degradation of contestability under increasing system velocity, with parameters calibrated against human-factors data on intervention latency . We propose a three-tier stress-test verification pipeline that formally distinguishes between identified, weakly identified, and non-identifiable agency dimensions. We develop a non-redundancy proposition establishing the informational distinctness of HAP from the existing WSNTTA vulnerability vector, and we specify a HAP-specific failure-mode register with operational detection procedures. The framework is positioned as a falsifiable research program with explicit integration gates, pre-registration instruments, and a scope clause that makes normative commitments explicit rather than implicit.

Keywords: human agency, meaningful human control, agency measurement, contestability, algorithmic governance, responsibility quantification, socio-technical systems, identification theory, technological transition.

