4. Contestation-Response Latency Function Family

4.1 The False-Gate Problem

As agentic automation scales, systems increasingly feature nominal human oversight while structurally stripping away the time, context, and cognitive bandwidth required to dissent. Bainbridge's "Ironies of Automation" established the foundational paradox: the more reliable an automated system becomes, the less practice the human operator gets, and the less capable they become of intervening when the system fails. The operator's role shifts from active controller to passive monitor, and passive monitoring degrades precisely the skills needed in rare, high-consequence moments.

4.2 Corrected Contestation Function

The first-version function had a sign problem: as written, C_H \to 0.5 when information accessibility I_H \to 0 and system velocity dominates. We replace it with:

C_H(t) = I_H(t) \cdot \sigma\!\left(-\frac{\tau_{\text{system}}(t)}{\tau_{\text{human}}(t)} \cdot \alpha\right)
\tag{4.1}

where:

· \sigma(\cdot) is the logistic function;
· I_H(t) \in [0,1] is information accessibility;
· \alpha > 0 is an asymmetry penalty capturing structural information disadvantages;
· \tau_{\text{system}}(t) is the system's execution velocity;
· \tau_{\text{human}}(t) is the time required for a human agent to review, challenge, and reverse an automated decision.

Limiting behavior:

· When \tau_{\text{system}} \gg \tau_{\text{human}} and I_H \to 0: C_H \to 0, correctly identifying the false gate.
· When \tau_{\text{system}} \ll \tau_{\text{human}} and I_H = 1: C_H \to 1, correctly identifying genuine contestability.
· When I_H is high but \tau_{\text{system}} \gg \tau_{\text{human}}: C_H \to I_H \cdot 0 = 0.

4.3 Human Review Time Model

\tau_{\text{human}} is not a fixed constant. It is a function of expertise, interface design, cognitive load, and practice:

\tau_{\text{human}}(t) = \tau_{\text{base}} \cdot \left(1 + \frac{\kappa_1}{\text{expertise}(t)}\right) \cdot \left(1 + \frac{\kappa_2}{\text{interface\_quality}(t)}\right) \cdot \left(1 + \frac{\kappa_3}{\text{cognitive\_load}(t)}\right)
\tag{4.2}

where \tau_{\text{base}} is the baseline reaction time. Human-factors literature suggests that between 5 and 8 seconds is a minimum time for takeover, with most studies examining response situations around 2 seconds . Manual takeover may require 15 to 40 seconds for the user to understand the situation and control it properly . These values inform the calibration of \tau_{\text{base}}.

4.4 Alternative Functional Forms

We carry three alternative forms as sensitivity cases:

Threshold form:

C_H^{\text{thresh}}(t) = I_H(t) \cdot \mathbb{1}\!\left[\frac{\tau_{\text{system}}(t)}{\tau_{\text{human}}(t)} < \theta_C\right]
\tag{4.3}

Piecewise-linear form:

C_H^{\text{lin}}(t) = I_H(t) \cdot \max\!\left(0, 1 - \beta \cdot \frac{\tau_{\text{system}}(t)}{\tau_{\text{human}}(t)}\right)
\tag{4.4}

Survival-model form:

C_H^{\text{surv}}(t) = I_H(t) \cdot \exp\!\left(-\lambda_C \cdot \frac{\tau_{\text{system}}(t)}{\tau_{\text{human}}(t)}\right)
\tag{4.5}

Rank correlation across forms is reported alongside all published C_H values.

4.5 Blind-Accept Rate as Primary Observable

The blind-accept rate (BAR) is a directly observable proxy for rubber-stamping:

\text{BAR}(t) = \frac{N_{\text{accepted without review}}}{N_{\text{total accepted}}}
\tag{4.6}

A review counts as substantive if any of the following conditions is met:

1. Review time exceeds \tau_{\text{review}} (declared per domain);
2. At least one edit or override attempt is made;
3. An independent evidence check is performed;
4. The reviewer documents a reason for disagreement before accepting.

This operational definition prevents the BAR from being gamed by superficial review actions. Automation-bias research shows that operators tend to blindly follow automation when the automation performs well, and this relationship is positive and linear .

4.6 Effective Oversight

O_H(t) is measured by three components:

O_H(t) = w_1 \cdot (1 - \text{BAR}(t)) + w_2 \cdot \text{latency-to-intervention}(t) + w_3 \cdot (1 - \text{asymmetry\_penalty}(t))
\tag{4.7}

where:

· latency-to-intervention is the time between system action and effective human counter-action;
· asymmetry_penalty captures the degree to which the system withholds or obscures decision-relevant context;
· w_1 + w_2 + w_3 = 1 are weights declared in the pre-registration.

A system with high nominal oversight and high BAR is structurally low-agency regardless of its formal design.

