5. Stress-Test Verification Pipeline

5.1 Purpose

Every candidate dimension in \mathcal{H}(t) must pass a three-tier validation protocol.

5.2 Tier 1 — Ex-Ante Audit Protocol

Evaluate whether procedural workflows permit meaningful refusal before irreversible downstream execution occurs.

Criterion. A system passes Tier 1 if there exists a decision point at which:

\tau_{\text{available}}(t) > \tau_{\text{review}}(t)
\tag{5.1}

where \tau_{\text{available}} is the time window available for human intervention and \tau_{\text{review}} is the minimum time required for substantive review.

5.3 Tier 2 — Counterfactual Perturbation Test

Introduce synthetic constraints into the simulation matrix to test whether the human agent can successfully alter system trajectories without incurring catastrophic institutional or economic penalties.

Criterion. A system passes Tier 2 if, under perturbation \delta, the human agent can achieve an alternative trajectory T' such that:

U(T') > U(T_{\text{system}}) - \kappa
\tag{5.2}

where U is a declared utility function defined over the human agent's objectives (not the system's objectives) and \kappa is a penalty threshold. The utility function must be specified in the pre-registration: it is the human agent's expected value of the alternative trajectory minus the expected cost of contestation. \kappa is calibrated to the domain: for medical AI, \kappa might be the expected increase in patient mortality; for financial AI, the expected loss in portfolio value.

5.4 Tier 3 — Non-Identifiability Screening

Formally prove whether an unobserved agency loss vector can be distinguished from standard operational variance using available telemetry.

Criterion. If the agency loss signal is non-identifiable under (3.1)–(3.4), the dimension is quarantined from \mathbf{X}^*(t) and reported as non-identifiable.

5.5 Synthetic Recovery Gate

Before Tier 1–3, every dimension must pass the synthetic recovery gate described in Section 3.5.

