6. The WSNTTA–HAP Integration Architecture

6.1 One-Way Principle

\boxed{ HAP\ \text{extends WSNTTA analysis without acquiring WSNTTA authority} }

HAP does not modify the core state vector \mathbf{X}(t) during initial development. It is represented as:

H(t) = \mathcal{H}\!\left[\mathbf{X}(t), I(t), G(t)\right]
\tag{6.1}

where I(t) is information conditions and G(t) is institutional conditions. Only after independent validation could a future WSNTTA revision consider an augmented state representation:

\mathbf{X}^*(t) = [\mathbf{R}(t), \mathbf{C}(t), \mathbf{D}(t), \mathbf{L}(t), \mathbf{V}(t), H(t)]
\tag{6.2}

6.2 Integration Gate

HAP Integration Gate. HAP may modify \mathbf{X}(t) to \mathbf{X}^*(t) only if:

1. the non-redundancy proposition (HAP-0.1) holds against \mathbf{V}(t);
2. the measurement model is identified under declared assumptions;
3. at least one HAP stress test has been pre-registered and executed with a bounded result;
4. the result has been independently replicated by a group with no WSNTTA donor funding.

Absent all four conditions, HAP remains a transversal analytical overlay, not a state-vector component.

6.3 Separability Condition

If I(t) and G(t) are derived from \mathbf{X}(t), then H(t) = \mathcal{H}'[\mathbf{X}(t)] and HAP is a function of the very transition state it is supposed to independently assess. To avoid this circularity, HAP declares a separability condition:

\mathcal{H}[\mathbf{X}, I, G] = \mathcal{H}_1(\mathbf{X}) \oplus \mathcal{H}_2(I \mid \mathbf{X}) \oplus \mathcal{H}_3(G \mid \mathbf{X})
\tag{6.3}

Where separability cannot be justified, HAP produces joint characterizations, not independent assessments. Separability is a pre-registration item: for every study, declare which components of I and G are treated as exogenous (or instrumented) and which are allowed to be joint with \mathbf{X}.

