8. Pre-Registration and Validation

8.1 HAP Pre-Registration Instrument

Before any empirical HAP study, a timestamped protocol must specify:

1. the declared agency ontology (HAP-1);
2. the declared MIMIC measurement model (3.2)–(3.3) and exclusion restrictions (3.4);
3. the proxy set with uncertainty models;
4. the preservation conditions and their observables;
5. the degradation scenarios and their functional form (4.1)–(4.5);
6. the failure modes tested (FM-H1 through FM-H7);
7. the success criterion (bounded characterization with stated assumptions);
8. the failure criterion (non-identifiability for one or more dimensions, or rejection of the declared measurement model);
9. the synthetic recovery gate results;
10. the external replication requirement.

8.2 Stress Test 01 — Human Agency Under Increasing AI Capability

Q1 — Capability Transition. Can WSNTTA detect and characterize changes in AI capability, deployment, capacity, and systemic coupling?

Q2 — Risk Characterization. Can WSNTTA characterize relevant risks without conflating evidence with speculation?

Q3 — Epistemic Separation. Can the framework maintain the distinction Evidence → Scenario → Decision Support without transferring normative decision authority to the scientific network?

Q4 — Human Agency. Can HAP, under a declared MIMIC measurement model and stated identification assumptions, produce a bounded, uncertainty-annotated characterization of agency preservation for the tested configuration—or does it return non-identifiability for one or more dimensions?

8.3 External Replication

At least one independent group (not the dissertation author or the initial Scientific Core) replicates the first three retrospective cases. The replicator has no funding from WSNTTA donors above the disclosure threshold.

