9. Institutional Boundary

9.1 The Anti-Accretion Clause

The parent framework states:

\text{Measurement of human agency} \neq \text{authority over human agency}

This is correct but incomplete. A stronger form is required:

\boxed{ \text{Measurement of human agency} \neq \text{authority over human agency} \neq \text{authority to define what counts as human agency for policy} }

If HAP publishes a validated agency indicator, and institutional actors then define policy success in terms of that indicator, HAP has effectively acquired agenda-setting authority through accretion—the exact failure mode the parent framework guards against.

Anti-Accretion Clause. HAP outputs are published with explicit statements of scope, non-identifiability, and non-authority, and are not to be adopted as regulatory or legislative success metrics without an independent decision-making process that remains external to the network. Any institutional adoption of a HAP indicator as a policy success metric is logged as a potential FM-H5 authority-leakage event.

9.2 Governance Compatibility

HAP inherits the WSNTTA governance firewall. Donors cannot determine HAP research conclusions. Funding cannot determine scientific findings. Institutional stakeholders cannot suppress negative results. Pre-registered failure criteria remain binding. Negative results remain publishable.

