10. Conclusion

10.1 The Foundational Principles

\boxed{ \text{No agency claim without identification.} }

\boxed{ \text{No integration without non-redundancy.} }

\boxed{ \text{No scalar without justification.} }

\boxed{ \text{No oversight without contestability.} }

10.2 What HAP Is

· A transversal theoretical module within WSNTTA.
· A vector-valued framework for agency measurement, grounded in the MHC tracking/tracing framework.
· A formal MIMIC identification program with exclusion restrictions and synthetic recovery gates.
· A calibrated family of degradation models for contestability.
· A falsifiable research program with operational failure modes.

10.3 What HAP Is Not

· A scalar agency index.
· A new governance authority.
· A replacement for WSNTTA.
· A normative doctrine disguised as measurement.
· A predictor of agency loss with certainty.

10.4 Closing

The HAP framework is architecturally correct and now formally grounded. The MHC mapping establishes its theoretical baseline; the MIMIC specification provides a concrete identification strategy; the calibrated latency family replaces the heuristic sketch; the synthetic recovery gate enforces identification discipline; and the operational failure-mode register makes the framework auditable. What remains is the first pre-registered stress test, the first synthetic recovery experiment, and the first public non-identifiability report—not further conceptual expansion.

The Network does not attempt to own the future. It attempts to make the conditions of technological transition sufficiently observable, testable, and transparent that humanity can remain capable of understanding and deciding what happens next.

