Appendix D — Measurement Model Specification (MIMIC)

D.1 Purpose

To specify the Multiple-Indicator Multiple-Cause (MIMIC) model for latent agency dimensions, including the structural equation, the measurement equations, identification conditions, and the synthetic recovery protocol.

D.2 Model Specification

The MIMIC model involves using latent variables that are predicted by observed variables and measured by multiple indicators . For HAP, this takes the form:

Structural equation:

H^*_{it} = \boldsymbol{\beta}_i^\top \mathbf{X}_t + \boldsymbol{\gamma}_i^\top \mathbf{Z}_t + \varepsilon_{it}, \qquad \varepsilon_{it} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_i^2)
\tag{D.1}

Measurement equations:

A_{ijt} = \lambda_{ij} H^*_{it} + \delta_{ijt}, \qquad \delta_{ijt} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \omega_{ij}^2)
\tag{D.2}

where H^*_{it} is the latent agency dimension i, \mathbf{X}_t is the WSNTTA transition state, \mathbf{Z}_t is the instrument vector, A_{ijt} is the j-th indicator of dimension i, and \lambda_{ij} is the factor loading.

D.3 Identification Conditions

For identification, we require:

1. At least two indicators per latent dimension (j \geq 2 for all i);
2. Normalization: set \lambda_{i1} = 1 for each dimension i (or set \psi_{ii} = 1);
3. Rank condition: the instrument matrix \mathbf{Z} must have rank q;
4. Exclusion restriction: \mathbf{Z}_t \perp \mathbf{A}_t \mid H^*_t, \mathbf{X}_t;
5. Overidentifying restrictions: where m_i > 2, the model provides testable overidentifying restrictions.

D.4 Exclusion Restriction

Assumption D.4.1 (Exclusion). There exist instruments \mathbf{Z}_t that affect H^*_t but affect \mathbf{A}_t only through H^*_t:

\mathbf{Z}_t \perp \mathbf{A}_t \mid H^*_t, \mathbf{X}_t
\tag{D.3}

The exclusion restriction requires that the instrument has no direct effect on the outcome except through the treatment.

D.5 Identification Status Classification

Every candidate dimension carries exactly one status:

\text{status}(H_i) \in \{\text{identified}, \text{weakly identified}, \text{non-identifiable}\}
\tag{D.4}

· identified: recoverable from observed data under declared assumptions;
· weakly identified: recoverable under additional parametric assumptions that cannot be fully tested;
· non-identifiable: cannot be distinguished from operational variance using available telemetry.

D.6 Synthetic Recovery Protocol

Before any observational study, each dimension must pass a synthetic recovery experiment:

1. Generate synthetic data from the MIMIC model (D.1)–(D.2) with known parameter values.
2. Estimate the model using the declared estimator (maximum likelihood or Bayesian).
3. Assess recovery: bias, RMSE, and coverage of confidence intervals for each parameter.
4. Report the recovery rate as a function of sample size, noise level, and instrument strength.
5. A dimension passes the recovery gate only if:
   · bias < 10\% of the true value for at least 80% of parameters;
   · 95% confidence interval coverage > 90\% for at least 80% of parameters.

If a dimension fails the synthetic recovery gate, it is marked non-identifiable and excluded from the headline state vector.

D.7 Estimator Specification

Estimator Use Case Software
Maximum likelihood (ML) Large samples, normal indicators lavaan, Mplus
Weighted least squares (WLSMV) Categorical indicators lavaan
Bayesian MCMC Small samples, prior information Stan, JAGS
Instrumental variable (2SLS) Endogenous causes ivreg, systemfit

D.8 Sensitivity to Unmeasured Confounding

For a linear Gaussian system with latent confounder U influencing both H^* and A, the bias bound is:

\widehat{\text{bias}} \leq \alpha_U \cdot \frac{\mathbb{V}[U]}{\mathbb{V}[H^*]}
\tag{D.5}

A sensitivity analysis reports \widehat{\beta} as a function of hypothetical \alpha_U values.

