Appendix F — Identification Protocols and Non-Identifiability Reporting

F.1 Purpose

To specify the protocols for identification testing, non-identifiability detection, and the reporting format for non-identifiable dimensions.

F.2 Identification Audit Protocol

For each dimension i, the audit proceeds in five steps:

1. Structural identification: verify that the MIMIC model (D.1)–(D.2) satisfies the rank condition and order condition.
2. Instrument validity: test the exclusion restriction via overidentification tests (Hansen J-test, Sargan test) where applicable.
3. Synthetic recovery: execute the synthetic recovery protocol (D.6).
4. Practical identification: assess whether the observed data provide sufficient information to estimate the parameters with acceptable precision.
5. Sensitivity analysis: report sensitivity of estimates to violations of the exclusion restriction.

F.3 Non-Identifiability Detection

Non-identifiability is detected via three complementary methods:

Method 1 — Variance-based sensitivity analysis. Variance-based sensitivity tools have limits for completely concluding on global non-identifiability of parameters . We use Sobol' indices as a screening tool, not as a definitive test. A dimension is flagged if all first-order Sobol' indices fall below a declared threshold.

Method 2 — Profile likelihood. For each parameter, construct the profile likelihood. If the profile likelihood is flat over a wide range, the parameter is non-identifiable.

Method 3 — Practical identifiability assessment. Assess the Fisher Information Matrix. If the smallest eigenvalue is below a declared threshold, the model is ill-conditioned and parameters may be non-identifiable .

F.4 Non-Identifiability Report Format

When a dimension is classified as non-identifiable, the following report is published:

Field Content
Dimension H_i
Status Non-identifiable
Detection method Which of F.3 methods flagged it
Diagnostic evidence Sobol' indices, profile likelihood plots, Fisher eigenvalues
Consequence Quarantined from \mathbf{X}^*(t)
Recommendation Data requirements that would enable identification
Re-evaluation trigger New data source, new instrument, structural break

Non-identifiable dimensions are reported as scientific findings, not as failures.

F.5 Sensitivity to Exclusion-Restriction Violations

For each instrument, report the estimated effect under the assumption that the exclusion restriction holds, and under a range of violation magnitudes. The sensitivity bound follows Conley et al. (2012):

\widehat{\beta}^{\text{adj}} = \widehat{\beta}^{\text{IV}} - \frac{\delta}{\pi}
\tag{F.1}

where \delta is the direct effect of the instrument on the outcome and \pi is the instrument strength.

