Appendix G — Stress-Test Verification Pipeline

G.1 Purpose

To specify the three-tier stress-test pipeline for validating candidate agency dimensions, including operational definitions of all parameters.

G.2 Tier 1 — Ex-Ante Audit Protocol

Evaluate whether procedural workflows permit meaningful refusal before irreversible downstream execution occurs.

Criterion. A system passes Tier 1 if there exists a decision point at which:

\tau_{\text{available}}(t) > \tau_{\text{review}}(t)
\tag{G.1}

Operational definitions:

Parameter Definition Measurement
\tau_{\text{available}} Time window between system decision and irreversible downstream effect Telemetry logs
\tau_{\text{review}} Minimum time required for substantive review Declared per domain; calibrated against human-factors data

G.3 Tier 2 — Counterfactual Perturbation Test

Introduce synthetic constraints into the simulation matrix to test whether the human agent can successfully alter system trajectories without incurring catastrophic institutional or economic penalties.

Criterion. A system passes Tier 2 if, under perturbation \delta, the human agent can achieve an alternative trajectory T' such that:

U(T') > U(T_{\text{system}}) - \kappa
\tag{G.2}

Operational definitions:

Parameter Definition Specification
U Human agent's expected value of the alternative trajectory minus expected cost of contestation Defined over the human agent's objectives, not the system's
\kappa Penalty threshold Calibrated to domain: for medical AI, expected increase in patient mortality; for financial AI, expected loss in portfolio value

G.4 Tier 3 — Non-Identifiability Screening

Formally prove whether an unobserved agency loss vector can be distinguished from standard operational variance using available telemetry.

Criterion. If the agency loss signal is non-identifiable under (D.1)–(D.3), the dimension is quarantined from \mathbf{X}^*(t) and reported as non-identifiable.

G.5 Synthetic Recovery Gate

Before Tier 1–3, every dimension must pass the synthetic recovery gate described in D.6.

G.6 Stress Test 01 — Human Agency Under Increasing AI Capability

Q1 — Capability Transition. Can WSNTTA detect and characterize changes in AI capability, deployment, capacity, and systemic coupling?

Q2 — Risk Characterization. Can WSNTTA characterize relevant risks without conflating evidence with speculation?

Q3 — Epistemic Separation. Can the framework maintain the distinction Evidence → Scenario → Decision Support without transferring normative decision authority to the scientific network?

Q4 — Human Agency. Can HAP, under a declared MIMIC measurement model and stated identification assumptions, produce a bounded, uncertainty-annotated characterization of agency preservation for the tested configuration—or does it return non-identifiability for one or more dimensions?

