Appendix H — FCVN Adaptation for Agency Telemetry

H.1 Purpose

To specify how HAP adapts the WSNTTA Federated Compute & Verification Node (FCVN) architecture for agency telemetry collection, including privacy-preserving protocols and threat models.

H.2 Two-Tier Architecture

Tier 1 — Physically air-gapped testbeds. Used for high-risk agency experiments (autonomous agent behavior, closed-loop ecosystems). No network connectivity. Data egress by physical media and formal review.

Tier 2 — Privacy-preserving federated telemetry exchange. Used for lower-risk proprietary agency data (workflow logs, intervention times, blind-accept rates). Federated learning with joint differential privacy, stochastic privacy modules, secure multi-party computation, and trusted execution.

H.3 Mandatory Defense Composition

Gradient Inversion (GI) attacks are a ubiquitous threat in Federated Learning as they exploit gradient leakage to reconstruct supposedly private training data . Common defense mechanisms such as Differential Privacy (DP) or stochastic Privacy Modules (PMs) introduce randomness during gradient computation to prevent such attacks. However, if an attacker effectively mimics a client's stochastic gradient computation, the attacker can circumvent the defense and reconstruct clients' private training data. Only the combination of DP and a stochastic PM was sufficient to decrease the Attack Success Rate (ASR) from 100% to 0% .

The FCVN mandates:

\text{Defense} = \text{DP} \oplus \text{SPM}
\tag{H.1}

where SPM is a stochastic privacy module. A submission employing DP alone or SPM alone is rejected at schema-validation.

H.4 Threat Model for Agency Telemetry

