Appendix K — Governance and Anti-Accretion

K.1 Purpose

To specify the governance firewall, the anti-accretion clause, and the institutional boundary conditions for HAP.

K.2 Anti-Accretion Clause

\boxed{ \text{Measurement of human agency} \neq \text{authority over human agency} \neq \text{authority to define what counts as human agency for policy} }

HAP outputs are published with explicit statements of scope, non-identifiability, and non-authority, and are not to be adopted as regulatory or legislative success metrics without an independent decision-making process that remains external to the network.

K.3 Authority-Leakage Detection

Any institutional adoption of a HAP indicator as a policy success metric is logged as a potential FM-H5 authority-leakage event. The detection protocol:

1. Monitor public policy documents for references to HAP indicators;
2. If a HAP indicator is cited as a success metric, file an authority-leakage report;
3. The report specifies: which indicator, which institution, what policy context;
4. The Scientific Core issues a public statement clarifying the non-authority status of HAP outputs.

K.4 Governance Firewall Rules

1. Donors cannot vote on scientific priorities.
2. Donors cannot nominate Scientific Directors.
3. Dual membership (donor governance + scientific governance) is prohibited.
4. Term limits apply to all scientific governance roles.
5. All budgets and flows are published annually.
6. Any breach triggers an external review with published findings.

K.5 Evidence–Scenario–Decision Pipeline

\boxed{
\mathbf{I}_H(t), \mathbf{C}_H(t), \mathcal{H}(t) \rightarrow \text{Evidence} \rightarrow \text{Scenario} \rightarrow \text{Decision Support}
}

HAP produces evidence, scenarios, and decision support. Legitimate authorities make decisions. This pipeline preserves scientific neutrality.

